ow to Vote Prep for the 2026 Statewide General Election General FAQs Elections & Statistics Election Results Upcoming Elections Rules for Municipal Elections Special Elections General Election Calendars Electoral College Election Audits Voter Registration and Participation Statistics Training Poll Manager Information Apply to be a Poll Manager Online Training Handbook & Resources Candidates Candidate Information Candidate Tracking System Campaign Finance & Ethics Political Parties Resources Precincts and Polling Places Precinct & District Maps Election Law Changes Municipal Election Commission Resources List Sales Help America Vote Act Vulnerability Disclosure Program Voter Education Materials Election Integrity in South Carolina Contact County Voter Registration & Election Offices Report Fraud State Election Commission Submit a Comment or Complaint FOIA Requests About the SEC Accountability Reports Annual Reports Meeting Minutes Procurement and Awards State Procurement Card Statements SEC Job Vacancies Public Notices SEC Website Privacy Statement Fines and Fees Collected FY2009 - FY2017 fines and fees collected are included in Accountability Reports . FY2018 - Fines and Fees Collected FY2019 - Fines and Fees Collected FY2020 - Fines and Fees Collected FY2021 - Fines and Fees Collected FY2022 - Fines and Fees Collected FY2023 - Fines and Fees Collected Requirements to Challenge a Ballot Poll managers, any voter, and any qualified watcher may challenge the vote of a person who may be known or suspected not to be a qualified voter. A challenge may also be made if you have good reason to believe the person has already voted or has become disqualified for any cause. Challenges of ballots cast in person in the absentee precinct and at the polling place on election day must be made prior to the voter casting the ballot. No challenges can be made after that time. All challenges must be addressed to poll managers and not directly to voters. Challenges of absentee by mail ballots must be made prior to separating the ballot from absentee return envelope, which can begin as early as 7:00 a.m. on the second day immediately preceding election day. Challenges in the absentee precinct are very rare because the voter has the opportunity to make address changes or otherwise correct his voting record with election officials before casting an absentee ballot. If a challenged voter casts a provisional ballot, the challenger must appear or produce evidence at the provisional ballot hearing to sustain the challenge; otherwise, the ballot will be counted. See S.C. Code sections 7-13-810 and 7-13-830. 2020 Election Fraud Investigations Report Commission Meetings Below are scheduled meetings of the State Election Commission and the State Board of Canvassers. All meetings, unless otherwise noted, will be held at 1122 Lady St., Suite 500, Columbia, SC, 29201. Notices are subject to change up until 24 hours prior to meeting. State Election Commission All meetings held at 10:00 a.m. unless noted otherwise. Meeting Schedule January 21, 2026 ( Agenda ) February 18, 2026 ( Agenda ) March 9, 2026 ( Agenda ) - This meeting will be held at 3 p.m. March 18, 2026 ( Agenda ) - This meeting will be held at 2 p.m. March 25, 2026 ( Agenda ) - This meeting will be held at 2 p.m. April 15, 2026 (Agenda) April 28, 2026 (Agenda) - This meeting will be held at 2 p.m. May 20, 2026 (Agenda) June 17, 2026 - Canceled July 15, 2026 (Agenda) July 20, 2026 ( Agenda ) - This meeting will be held at 9:00 a.m. August 28, 2026 ( Agenda ) - This meeting will be held immediately following the State Board of Canvassers meeting. September 16, 2026 - Canceled October 21, 2026 November 18, 2026 December 16, 2026 State Board of Canvassers (as needed) All meetings held at 3:00 p.m. unless noted otherwise. October 24, 2025 for State Senate District 12 and State House of Representatives Districts 21 and 88 Special Primaries ( Agenda ) November 7, 2025 for State House of Representatives District 98 Special Election, State House District 21 Special Runoff and State House District 88 Special Republican Primary ( Agenda ) November 21, 2025 for State House of Representatives District 98 Republican Special Primary Election Runoff ( Agenda ) - Meeting to be held at 2 p.m. December 30, 2025 for State Senate District 12 and State House of Representatives Districts 21 and 88 Special Elections ( Agenda ) - Meeting to be held at 2 p.m. January 9, 2026 for State House of Representatives District 98 Special Election ( Agenda ) - Meeting to be held at 2 p.m. June 12, 2026 for Certification of Results from June 9 Primary (Agenda) - Meeting to be held at 2 p.m. Signed Canvass Sheets June 26, 2026 for Certification of Results from June 23 Primary Runoffs (Agenda) - Meeting to be held at 2 p.m. Signed Canvass Sheets August 14, 2026 for Certification of Results from August 11 U.S. Senate Special Republican Primary (Agenda) - Meeting to be held at 11:00 a.m. Signed Canvass Sheets August 28, 2026 for Certification of Results from August 11 U.S. Senate Special Republican Primary Runoff ( Agenda ) - Meeting to be held at 11:00 a.m. Signed Canvass Sheets September 4, 2026 for Certification of Results from September 1, 2026 State Senate District 15 Special Democratic Primary ( Agenda ) \u2013 Meeting to be held at 11:00 a.m. Signed Canvass Sheets Meeting Minutes MyscVOTES Check My Registration Get My Sample Ballot Find My Polling Place View My Voting History Log in to MyscVOTES Find Your County Voter Registration & Elections Office Find Yours 3,448,376 Current number of registered voters in South Carolina South Carolina Election Commision Every Vote Matters. Every Vote Counts. NoExcuseSC.gov All logos and trademarks in this site are property of their respective owner. \u00a9 2026 South Carolina State Election Commission. All Rights Reserved. Resources Register to Vote Be a Poll Manager News Early Voting - 2026 Statewide General Election Upcoming Elections Contact Absentee Voting